
---------------------------------------------------------------
  Wzonka-Lad - Yet Another Gameboy Emulator for 68020+ Amigas  
---------------------------------------------------------------
           Written by Ville Helin in 1996-1999
 For the latest sources: https://github.com/vhelin/wzonka-lad


1. BACKGROUND

This is a Gameboy emulator written completely in 68020 asm.
This used to be shareware, but it is now (after 11-Aug-2003)
GPL 2 software.


2. LEGAL STUFF

Wzonka-Lad is GPL 2 software. Read the LICENSE file for
further information. You use Wzonka-Lad at your own risk.
I take no responsibility for anything.


2. CHANGELOG (RECENT)

v1.12.00 (21-Nov-2026):

* Joypad interrupts follow falling edges on the selected Game Boy input
  lines, avoiding a spurious request in Blargg's halt bug test
* FULL checks for pending interrupts after a write to IF or IE, before
  fetching the next opcode. This addresses Blargg's CPU interrupt case 02
* The CGB speed-switch register (KEY1) reads as $FF in DMG emulation,
  preventing Blargg's CPU test 03 from getting stuck in STOP
* DMG sound length counters now survive NR52 power cycles and accept
  length-only writes while the APU is off (Blargg sound tests 08 and 11)
* DMG audio registers now return the hardware read masks (Blargg sound
  test 01). Write-only values are retained for playback and GBS snapshots
* DMG audio register writes and length counters keep working when AHI is
  unavailable, sound is off, or individual playback channels are muted
* FULL clocks DMG sound lengths from the emulated divider, including
  DIV resets, and handles the extra length clocks on enable and trigger
  writes. FAST and WARP retain scanline-based length timing
* FULL clocks channel 1 frequency sweep from the same divider as length,
  even with host audio muted. Sweep pace reloads and negate-mode changes
  now follow DMG hardware (Blargg sound tests 05 and 07)
* FULL emulates active DMG wave RAM reads, writes and retrigger corruption
  (Blargg sound tests 09, 10 and 12). Wave timing is calculated on demand;
  FAST and WARP keep their existing wave RAM behavior
* FULL advances TIMA from the emulated divider, not from scanline sweeps.
  Timer reloads and IRQs now occur even when masked in IE. This fixes
  the timer calibration failure in Blargg's instruction timing test.
* FULL charges five machine cycles for interrupt entry, whether the IRQ
  is accepted inside a CPU slice or by the scanline scheduler.
* DMG startup recreates the boot logo tiles and map, mirrors WRAM into
  Echo RAM, and returns $FF from unused I/O, including $FF50.
  Cold boot now fills WRAM with a varied pattern without overwriting
  cartridge save RAM.
* FULL starts with the DMG divider phase and the end of VBlank. Scanlines
  budget 114 machine cycles at default speed and carry instruction overrun
  into the next slice.
* Keep the display blank while LCDC is off and through the first frame
  after re-enabling it, without suppressing guest VBlank or CPU timing.
* Poll host controllers at even intervals across frame boundaries, so
  joypad interrupts aren't locked to a few scanlines (Telling LYs test).
* Full GFX latches the Game Boy window when LY equals WY at the start
  of a scanline and advances its tile row only when visible.
* FULL restarts LCD timing on line zero after LCDC is re-enabled and
  samples STAT mode at the CPU's I/O read cycle (STATcount-auto).
  Mode 3 still uses a fixed baseline duration; sprite, window and
  scroll stalls are not timed individually. FAST and WARP are unchanged.
* FULL wraps instruction fetches at the 16-bit PC boundary. BullyGB can
  display its failure report instead of staying blank on the unsupported
  DMA bus-conflict test.
